            <ArticleTitle>[Cardiosurgical therapy of coronary heart disease in terminal kidney insufficiency].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>788-92</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine retrospectively the complications and long-term results of aortocoronary bypass grafting in patients with end-stage renal disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">65 patients with coronary heart disease (CHD) and on dialysis (54 men, 11 women; average age 56.9 +/- 8.1 years) underwent aortocoronary bypass grafting between 1982 and 1992. Mean duration of dialysis (haemo- or peritoneal) was 41.1 +/- 45.0 (1-215) months. All patients had had haemofiltration treatment in conjunction with the bypass operation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Coronary angiography demonstrated triple-vessel disease in 40 patients (62%). Average number of bypasses was 2.8 per patient. Perioperative death rate was 4.6%. 95% of survivors were free of symptoms 6 months postoperatively. Long-term survival rate was 71% after 3 years and 55% after 5 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Aortocoronary bypass grafting for CHD in patients with end-stage renal disease can be performed with a low perioperative mortality rate and significantly improves symptoms.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
